- `precedence(n4, c1)` & `precedence(c1, n5)`: The source states that selecting the alternative path should "proceed to order dispatch", but the model forces it to merge at `c1` and continue to `n5` ("Check Material Availability"), violating the documented chronology and path progression (SMC3, SMC5).
- `precedence(b2, c2)`: The source specifies that if goods are available, "a manufacturing order is placed", but the model omits this step by directly linking `b2` to `c2` (SMC1).
- `precedence(n8, n9)` & `precedence(n9, n10)`: The source explicitly states that updating stock and dispatching motorcycles happen "At the same time" / "Simultaneously", but the model sequences them linearly via `n8` → `n9` → `n10` without a concurrency gateway (SMC4).
- `defines(v1, "Production Order Received?", {})` & `defines(v2, "Check Material Availability", {})`: The value ranges are empty `{}`, failing to represent the implicit decision options (e.g., received/not received, available/unavailable) described in the text (SMC6).
- `branch(b1, XOR)` & `branch(b2, XOR)`: No `guard` statements connect these branches to `v1` and `v2`, leaving the decision criteria undefined relative to the declared variables (SMC4).

Structural Check
- [S1] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `[]`. | Variable `v2` has an invalid range `[]`.
- [S2] STC16 failed: Every outgoing edge of XOR/OR branches must have exactly one guard. | XOR/OR edge `b1 -> n3` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b1 -> n4` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> n6` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> c2` has 0 guards; exactly one is required.